Clear the Windows monitor owner on release, and keep the owner check race-free.

GMonitor::enter() decides without the lock whether the calling thread
already owns the monitor, by comparing `locker` with its own thread id.
The Windows leave() kept the last owner's id in `locker` after releasing
the critical section, so that thread could take the lock-free path while
another thread re-acquired the monitor in wait() and had not yet stored its
own id. Both threads then ran inside the monitor, and the next ownership
check threw GThreads.not_acq_*; from GMonitorLock's destructor that ends in
std::terminate. The POSIX leave() already resets `locker`.

`count` and `locker` are read without the lock, so they are atomic: a
thread can see its own id in `locker` only after storing it itself and
before clearing it, which makes the lock-free check exact.

--- a/libdjvu/GThreads.cpp
+++ b/libdjvu/GThreads.cpp
@@ -236,6 +236,7 @@
   if (count > 0)
     {
       count = 1;
+      locker = 0;
       if (ok)
         LeaveCriticalSection(&cs);
     }
--- a/libdjvu/GThreads.h
+++ b/libdjvu/GThreads.h
@@ -107,6 +107,7 @@
 #define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
 #include "windows.h"
 #endif
+#include <atomic>
 #endif
 
 #if POSIXTHREADS
@@ -269,8 +270,8 @@
 private:
 #if WINTHREADS
   int ok;
-  int count;
-  DWORD locker;
+  std::atomic<int> count;
+  std::atomic<DWORD> locker;
   CRITICAL_SECTION cs;
   struct thr_waiting *head;
   struct thr_waiting *tail;
